[TITLE -- GM'S CALL]
A Chronicle of the Obsidian Lanterns

PART I: THE HAND ON THE DOOR
The Crash
The den went quiet the way a room goes quiet after a bell. Wind screamed through the gap beyond the overhang. Inside, the black sap crusted against the ice walls of Ismara's casing and the troll lay where it had folded, cooling in the red slush.

Bjorn was on his knees near the mouth of the cave with what was left of Mable held against his chest. He made no sound. Mila stood beside him with her arms around his neck.

Saijah went to them. She was still lit from the inside, the purple and green still drifting at the edges of her sight, and she crossed the slush with her arms already opening to put them around the old man.

Mila put a hand up.

It was a small hand, flat, held out at the height of Saijah's belt, and it stopped her the way a drawn blade would have. Mila did not say anything. She had spent eight years in the Warrens watching a bottle take her mother apart a piece at a time, and she had now watched the woman whose hand she held through the sewers drink from the same kind of bottle, twice, in front of her. She kept her arm out and her other arm around Bjorn's neck and looked at Saijah until Saijah stopped.

Somebody in the company said she ought to throw the rest of the skooma into the snow. Saijah's hand went to her pack and stayed there. She kept all of it.

The Wall
It was Ismara who took her by the arm and started steering her away from the old man and the child, back toward the rear of the den. Saijah went a few steps, and then the smell reached her.

It came from the back wall -- the seamless one, the stone she'd marked in the middle of the fight as belonging to some other mountain. It was a hunting smell. It went past the skooma and past the ache and into the part of her that had always known how to track, and it pulled. She slipped Ismara's grip with a quick stutter-step, flitted across the den, and laid her palm flat on the stone.

The noise stopped.

For five seconds the ringing that had lived behind her eye since the Eldergleam simply ended. So did the skooma. So did the ache. Her pulse dropped to the slow, even beat of a stone in a stream. Her focus closed down into something velvet and absolute, and she could breathe the way a person breathes after years of breathing through a wet cloth.

In that silence, a voice brushed the base of her skull.

"Little sentinel. See how quiet it gets? That ringing in your head -- I can make it stay like this. I remember my first hunt on this mountain. I know the words the rock is waiting for. Offer the hawk talon to me. Give it to me, and the door opens."

He was patient about the second part. He added it the way a trader adds a sweetener to a price that hasn't moved yet: give him the talon, and she could take as much skooma as she wanted, and it would cost her nothing.

Ylva smelled him before anyone else knew he was there. Her fur came up along her shoulders and her lips peeled back off her teeth.

"He was right here," Ylva said. "What did He say to you?"

Saijah told her. The talon for the words. The talon, and all the skooma she wanted, with no price on it.

"Then give it to Him. Don't fucking waste it, you ungrateful bitch."

Ylva turned and walked away across the den.

Saijah stood with her hand on the stone for a long moment, in the quiet, weighing it. Then she took the hand back.

"Kynareth gave me these things," she said. "They've allowed me to get where I am right now."

She felt him smile as he went -- amused, unhurried, a hunter who has watched quarry pick the harder trail. The instant his grip left her, everything he had been holding back came down at once. The skooma crash and the goddess's unbuffered pressure hit her nervous system together, and she dropped to her knees in the slush. Her hands shook. Her stomach turned over. Sweat came up cold across her back. Somewhere past the edge of the pain, Ylva spat into the drift and walked away again.

Then a thin gust of mountain wind drew across her forehead, and with it came a knowing that did not arrive as words. Someone in the company already had the passphrase written down.

She said so out loud. Across the den, Ismara reached into her satchel for the rubbing she had taken off the waystone on the stag's stair.

The Care of the Body
Saijah did what she could for herself. She ate snowberries and dried mushrooms and drank cold water, the small useless kindnesses a person gives a body that is going to hurt regardless. They did nothing for the crash. She did them anyway.

Orion and Nora
Orion found Nora sitting against the rock where the troll had put her down.

"You okay, baby?"

"It kind of hurts," Nora said. "That's fine."

"That looked like it hurt a lot."

"I wish I wouldn't die so much."

"That's what I want to talk to you about," Orion said. "Because I don't want you to die either."

He started her on the plainest of his songs -- the horn's basic swell, the one that lifts every ally in earshot, the first thing any student of his would learn. She got her breath back and sang it with him. It took.

The Rubbing
They spread the rubbing out on a flat stone by the back wall and worked at it. Six words. Letters they half-recognized, arranged in a grammar none of them owned. It was the older script Atmoran had grown out of. Esbern leaned over it with trembling fingers and named it: pre-First Empire dragon script, the tongue the Greybeards were said to breathe.

The debate went back and forth at the wall. Ismara did not want the monks. Orion kept coming back to the same plain point: the only men alive who read this sat fifteen minutes up the path, and nothing stood in the way of reaching them now.

PART II: THE CARTER'S ROAD
Which Way
In the middle of it, some of them looked up and saw that the den was emptier than it had been.

Bjorn was standing against Balthazzar's flank. He had been wrapping a wool blanket around what was left of Mable, and his hands had stopped. The hemp rope slid through his torn, bloody fingers. His forehead was pressed to the mare's shoulder and his breath came short and fast.

Mila got there before any of them. She was taller now than she had been at the bottom of the Steps -- the mountain had carried her all the way to sixteen -- and faster, and she took the loose cord in both hands and pulled the knot tight. She folded the corner of the blanket down over Mable's head. Then she slid Balthazzar's lead rope out of Bjorn's fingers and looked up at him.

"Which way are we going, Bjorn?"

"Morthal. Down to the marsh. Soft earth there. We'll dig."

He took the rope back from her and started toward the mouth of the cave.

The Arguments
Orion reached for Calm. It broke against the old man and slid off; Bjorn did not want anything done to him.

Saijah followed him across the den and cast grand healing into the ground near his feet, wide enough to take in him and Mila and the mare together. Bjorn went rigid when it landed. He did not turn around.

"Grief makes us lost," Ismara said, "in every sense of the word."

Bjorn stopped.

"What do you know about grief?"

"Centuries."

"You grieve civilizations. Advancement. Progress." He still had not turned. "That's not the thing I'm grieving right now. I don't know if you can understand what I'm grieving, Ismara. I think Davinia could. I don't think you can. And that's not a dig at you. But don't pretend to understand things you don't."

He stood at the lip of the den with the rope in his hand.

"All I want to do right now is go and bury her. I want to take her home."

Ismara knelt in the slush by the blanket. Ice rose up around what was left of Mable in clean, deliberate sheets and closed into a casket, clear enough to see the grey wool through. She worked Mable's name into the lid, and a hoofprint beneath it, and let a fleck of gold drift through the ice like dust in sunlight. From her satchel she took a small braid of dried grass and set it on top before the last sheet sealed.

"I may not know grief," she said. "But I know you are grieving. I would never wish this on you. I don't want you to lose anything else on your journey. I want you to stay."

The others spoke to his back. They told him he had spent months telling them to keep the people they loved alive -- that this was what they were trying to do for him now. They told him that Mila walked where he walked, and that seven thousand steps of whiteout with cultists still on the road was the crossfire he'd sworn to keep her out of. That the marsh would still be there, and the soft ground, and the ash trees.

Orion said the last of it.

"Everything Mable did was to help us get here. She was a Lantern. I don't want her death to be for nothing."

Bjorn stood at the lip of the drop with his boots in the drift and the rope loose in his fingers. His shoulders came down.

They went to him, all of them, and put their arms around him -- Mila in the middle of it with the stuffed rabbit Ismara had bought her still in one fist. The old man cried. He cried the way a man does who has not done it where anyone could see since before most of them were born, and he did not try to stop.

He had never had a company. The War-Hound had cleared forts alone. He had been married, once, briefly; that was the only other time. In the circle of them he stood and wept for an old horse, and none of them let go first.

When he could speak, he looked at the ice.

"Keep her cold. Don't let the rot take her."

"Nothing touches her," Ismara said, "as long as I'm here."

Courage
Orion went back to Nora after, while the company was still finding its feet. He laid Courage on her before they started, and they sang the horn-song through again, and this time it sat in her the way it sat in him. One more long session, and she would have it for good.

PART III: THE WORDS
The Walk Up
They went back up to High Hrothgar, because Orion kept asking what reason there was not to, and nobody had one worth fifteen minutes of wind.

Esbern lengthened his stride until he was walking beside Ismara.

"Your notation is incredible. Where did you study?"

"A little here, a little there. Some schools I've forgotten the names of." She thought about it. "Cyrodiil. Bravil. And once I even managed to make it out to study in the Summerset Isles."

He was a tall man for his years, still built like the fighter he'd been before he was a scholar, and he walked the rest of the way up beside her talking alchemy and notation. She let him. He knew a little alchemy -- rudimentary, and eager -- and she answered his questions and did not look at him more than she had to.

Arngeir
The Greybeards were kneeling exactly where the company had left them. Arngeir took the rubbing, read it, and gave it back.

"The Voice is worship. Follow the Inner path."

The words would not open anything in the common tongue. They had to be spoken as written, in the old language, at the door itself, and by every voice in the company at once. He had never opened it. His brothers do not speak.

Saijah left a handful of snowberries by the empty hearth, and they went back down.

The Harness
On the way, Ismara drew GEAR aside.

"Hello, little one. I've been tinkering with an idea. Can you help me with it?"

"Perhaps I can. I am made to be a laboratory assistant. What is the experiment?"

It was less an experiment than a gift. She had pulled gears and springs out of the monastery's forgotten stores, and she worked them into a harness around his base on the pattern of her own enchanted boots -- the same grip, built out of metal instead of magic. She hammered and fitted and set it true at every joint. When she was done, GEAR rolled across the ice of the path and did not slide. On a mountain that had already dropped him over one edge, he would not fall off another.

Varon's Face
Orion fell in beside Varon.

"This adventure has been quite perturbing to me," Varon said. "We've aged significantly. It's especially bad for me."

"How?"

"This isn't my face. I am not supposed to wear it this long." A few years, the surgeons had meant; the mountain had put twenty on him. His body was healing around the borrowed bone and the reshaped orbits, sealing the disguise in. The procedure could only be done in the marshes of Black Marsh, with hist sap to survive it, and the sap did not travel. "The faster we finish our mission, the faster I can get back to what I have to do."

Orion told him he still looked like a normal person.

"I am not a person. I am an elf."

"You still look like an elf."

"No," Varon said. "I don't."

"You don't scare me, is what I'm saying."

Orion asked if he had any plans. Varon asked which part he meant, and walked on ahead without waiting for the answer.

Ylva on Saijah
Orion drifted back to Ylva next. She did not wait for a greeting.

"That fucking little bitch up there. Can you even believe her? Short-ass, scaredy little thing with her little satchel. She thinks she's so special because that assassin has a crush on her. She doesn't have to work for anything. It all gets handed to her on a silver platter, and she goes, oh, I'm too good for that, and flies around being a little angel of death."

Orion started laughing, and she kept going over the top of it. Saijah thought she was better than everybody. Better than Orion. Better than Alfonso. Better than the robot. When the talk turned to vampires, Ylva said her people and his had some history, and didn't feel like getting into it.

Orion walked away slowly. Behind him, she was still going.

The Curriculum
Before they reached the den, Ismara took Esbern aside with a proposition of her own. There was a young lady in the company who needed educating. Could he handle that?

"I can handle a curriculum," Esbern said.

Mila was sixteen in body and mind, and she had the schooling of a child raised in a sewer. Esbern watched Ismara walk away from the arrangement for a good while after she'd gone.

GEAR's Cadence
There was one problem with speaking in unison. GEAR could say the words, but not the way anyone else said them. His vocal apparatus kept its own timing and could not be made to keep theirs.

So they kept his. They practiced four at a time off to one side of the den, trading places until everyone had it, matching the clipped machine rhythm of the automaton syllable by syllable -- "Tah. Joor. Nir. Thu'um. Ven. Su'um." -- until the whole company spoke like GEAR.

Alfonso came back into himself in the middle of it, rising out of whatever skeleton-stillness he'd been keeping, and learned it last.

The Door
They gathered at the wall together: Ismara, Orion, Nora, Saijah, Alfonso, Ylva, Varon, Esbern, Bjorn, Mila, GEAR. Balthazzar stood among them. Orion counted them in.

The words left their mouths at once, in GEAR's rhythm, and the hollow of the cave caught them and threw them back. For a moment nothing happened.

Then a deep, resonant tone came up through the floorstones -- the mountain itself unlocking. The solid wall shivered and went translucent, like thin ice over water, and parted down the center. Hot air rolled out of the gap, heavy with minerals. The packed snow at the threshold slumped into water at once, hissing against the warm stone and running away down the rock.

PART IV: THE VAULT OF THE RETURN
The Treasury
Ismara cast Detect Life before anyone stepped through. Nothing answered. The door had been shut since before she existed.

The steps took them down into an expanse cut straight out of the mountain's dark living basalt, directly beneath the den where Mable died. Pillars of polished sea-stone and mammoth ivory climbed fifty feet into the dark, holding up arches braced with beaten bronze. Along the walls, carved panoramas: Green Atmora at the dawn, sunlit coasts and tundra and timber halls under a golden sky, before the Frostfall came. Ivory friezes of the Landing at Hsaarik Head and the Five Hundred Companions, and the names of the longships cut in the sharp wedge-runes Ysgramor invented -- Jorrvaskr. Krilot Lok. Kaal Kaaz. Fallowfire.

Ylva stopped in front of them and did not move.

She had been a Companion. This was the beginning of the thing she had been thrown out of, the first hall of it, carved by the hands that built it, and it had been sealed in the dark for longer than there had been a Dragon Cult to seal anything. She stood before the ivory with her hands open at her sides.

Esbern had a pen out before his feet reached the floor, and did not stop writing.

Ismara found Varon among the pillars and took his hand. He held very still. His palm was slick with sweat.

The Chests
Rows of stone chests stood on black basalt plinths: petrified scrolls, bronze tablets, sea-axes, and the totems of the old gods -- the Hawk of Kyne crowning the sky-vault, the Whale of Stuhn, the Bear of Tsun, the Wolf of Mara, all carved before the priests of the dragons ever touched the animal pantheon. Nothing in them would take an edge anymore. There was gold, a great deal of it, minted before the Empire existed; they might as well have been carrying the coin of a country at the bottom of the sea.

Somewhere between the chests the company agreed, without anybody quite proposing it, that they were not going to tell the Greybeards about any of this. Or anyone.

In the warm mineral runoff along the floor, Ismara found blisterwort caps and blue mountain flower growing wild in the heat, and knew the pair on sight. She took enough of each for three draughts.

The Journal
On a stone bench near a pair of bronze doors lay a leather journal, warped and water-stained through its first half. The last pages were crisp, the ink dark and steady.

Last of the tallow burned out hours ago. Fingers gone black at the tips. The Pale Lady's crypt is empty -- everyone fled when the sword was moved and they barred the outer grate behind them. Left me in the dark. Three days crawling the frost-rifts. Ate dried lichen off the barrow floor. The cold isn't an absence; it has teeth. Prayed to Arkay until my throat cracked. No warmth. No light. Nobody answers.

Then a stretch of blank leaves, scratched faintly. Then the hand changed -- sharp, flowing, feverishly exact.

How blind we were! Clinging to our little sacks of warm water and bone, shivering in our holes, begging the gods to let us keep rotting for another winter. The Master pulled me from a snow-drift in Winterhold. He did not give me bread. He gave me the fire that cleanses the slate! The cold was never the enemy -- the cold was only the fear of being unmade. Once you understand that the mountain itself wants to burn, the hunger stops. The dread stops. There is only the dawn.

The work at the root goes smoothly. Five great needles of volcanic glass -- the anchors of the First Father -- driven into the dragon's throat to pin the wound open. The master sings the loop, and the stone obeys. Five anchors form the circle, but three keystones hold the tension true. If three shatter, the whole glorious knot unravels and the fire spills across the sky!

Let them come with their swords. When the dawn breaks, even their steel will melt into the sea.

It was signed in a hand some of them knew. Jasper Avalon. They had left him dead in the Pale Lady's tomb.

The Arrow
Saijah went looking for somewhere to speak to Kynareth. She stood under the carved hawk and said what she had.

"I hope you never doubted my loyalty to you."

A sunbeam had found its way down through a crack somewhere far above and lay across an offering font beneath the hawk. Something in the font caught the light. She reached in and lifted out a single arrow -- pale petrified mountain ash, tipped in sky-tempered meteoric iron, fletched with storm-hawk feathers that did not ruffle or bend when she turned it in her hand.

The Dakhma
Alfonso wandered, and something drew him to one part of the vault in particular: a raised circular terrace of basalt, open above to the updrafts and the high fissures of the sky. A funeral altar. The carvings along its rim showed a dead hunter laid bare on the stone, storm-hawks and ravens and pack-wolves coming down to feed, fungus and beetles and rot taking the rest back into the mountain, and the bones left clean and white under the sun.

He worked out the wedge-runes cut above the dais.

The breath is the gift. The meat is the borrow. The hawk takes the eye, the wolf takes the flesh, the rot clears the stone. When the bone is clean, the man is free.

In a bronze-bound chest on the dais lay heavy slate tablets -- the whole liturgy of the thing, and beside the prayers, a formula: a fungus grown on geothermal heat, used to hold decaying matter steady. He took them.

The Stele
Orion found a carved stone set deep into a basalt recess and wrapped in iron chains. The chains ran taut between iron pegs driven into the rock at measured intervals, torqued tight, the same system that holds the strings to the bridge of a lute. The stone beneath was worn in circles where something heavy had struck the links, over and over, for a very long time.

Nora came up beside him and flicked one of the chains with a fingernail. It rang -- a clean, sustained pitch that filled the alcove and hung there longer than iron has any right to hold a note. She drew her hand back and looked at him.

He played it.

Then he read the runes, the parts the centuries had left legible:

The master tells the fledgling: your blood is mine. It is a lie. A fledgling who has fed on his own kill and kept his own will answers to no sire. The blood belongs to the body it beats in. He is no longer a thrall -- he is a master without subjects.

He took a rubbing, and he and Nora bent over it together and worked out the rest. The skalds of the Five Hundred had broken the rings and amulets Molag Bal used to bind his broods. Find the counter-tone, strike it into the metal, and the ring snaps -- and the spell sends a shockwave back up the thread to whoever holds the other end.

Nora's face lit. "We have to do this right now. Let's break it off you so he doesn't take your other hand."

In Orion's head, a voice he had never heard before spoke, flat and certain.

"Do not break it. The ring is mine. He borrowed it. You will give it back to me when I tell you to, and not before. Obey."

Fuck you, Orion thought.

He looked at Nora for a moment. Then he turned away from the chains. "Let's go look at these rocks. Look how old this is."

"Wait -- what? Hold on --"

He was already walking.

Davinia
Bjorn found Ismara at her notes, her hair up, deep in whatever she and GEAR and Esbern had been comparing. He asked her whether the offer still stood -- whether he could speak to Davinia.

Ismara let her hair down. She folded her glasses and tucked them away. The frost began to draw back off her skin, up her arms and her neck and her face, drawing back into the Crown, and the silver went with it. Her hair darkened to black. The face underneath was older than it had been at the bottom of the mountain.

The Davinia who came up was one Ismara had chosen: raised on the memory of the argument with her father before she left home, the day her mother had stood up for her. She looked around the vault, and at her own clothes, and at her own hands. On the back of one of them, the ember-mark Alfonso had burned into her in the monastery glowed faintly, and did not go out.

Bjorn sat down heavily on a basalt step. His breath came ragged again.

"Davinia. Look at my hands. They aren't my hands. These are my hands from thirty years ago. My hips don't ache anymore. What is this place doing to me? Can you tell me what this is?"

"I -- I can't," she said. "But I know who can. One second."

She asked, inside, where no one else could hear. Ismara answered.

Calm down. Just give him a little comfort, and we'll discuss this later. Stretch your legs. Hear his story. I'll be back.

Davinia sat down beside him on the step. She took off her cloak and put it around both of them, and put her arm around the old man, and stayed there. Her eyes kept moving the whole time -- the doors, the stairs, the dark between the pillars, where the exits were, where anything could come from. Nothing came.

"This is new," she said. "This is different."

She looked down at her own hands, the way he was looking at his.

The Wolf and the Rot
Ylva found Alfonso at the dakhma.

"Look at you. Decided to stand on your own feet again. Good."

"I'm kind of digging the whole skeleton thing."

She rested her hand on his forearm and leaned her weight against him, easy, as though there had never been any distance between them, and looked up at the carvings.

"So what is this place? I see the wolves carved all over it. What did the First Men build it for?"

"I think it describes what they did with their dead. Instead of burying them or burning them, they'd leave them out to return to the earth. That's a guess on my part."

"But why the wolves and birds?"

"I'm assuming those are the animals they paid tribute to. Also a guess." He told her the inscription, the breath and the borrow and the rot clearing the stone.

She was quiet for a moment.

"Back when I was a Companion," Ylva said, "they told me my appetite made me a monster. We treated the beast in our blood like a burden to be ashamed of. Like I was sick." She looked at the stone. "So. The wolf and the rot, you say. You believe the old ones had it right?"

"I like to think they did. I see us in this."

"You know what? Yeah." Her grip on his arm tightened. "You clean the bone. I'll tear the meat. We can hunt together."

He told her they hadn't talked in a while -- that part of him wanted to apologize for dying, not because he was sorry, but because he wanted to talk to her.

"You're so dumb," Ylva said. "I wasn't mad at you for dying. You're dead anyway. But you can't talk to me if you don't have a body, you dumb idiot."

"Yeah. I guess I'm a little stupid."

She laughed, and stayed close.

Esbern at the Frieze
Esbern found Saijah at the ivory with the new arrow still in her hand.

"Look at these names. Kaal Kaaz. Fallowfire. Five hundred mortals took an entire continent from the dragons and the elves and every kind of beast, and carved out a home from the rock and the ice with nothing but iron and blood and grit. For thirty years I thought the Blades were at an end. I was wrong. High King Torygg gave you a charter because the dragons are back. Skyrim needs the Companions again. That's what you all are, don't you see? We've got to get you working together."

"Tell me how to do that," Saijah said.

"I've been here a week. You know them better than I do."

The Armor
Mila found Davinia with a cuirass clutched to her chest. The mountain had split her child's clothes at every seam.

"Davinia. I grew. It's stuck. The buckles won't reach around my back and the shoulders keep falling down. How do you make it stay on?"

Davinia knew her at once. She took off her cloak again and wrapped it around the girl, and worked the old bronze buckles and the crossing straps until the carved Nord armor sat right on her. Then she went looking for a weapon, because a girl in armor ought to have one. Everything in the vault had rusted past use except a spear of worked stone, and she put that in Mila's hands.

"Let's go find Alfonso," Davinia said.

"I don't want to see Saijah right now."

"What's wrong with Saijah?"

"She's been taking skooma. It's scary."

The Pain
Before they reached him, Varon stepped out from between the ivory pillars onto the terrace, as though he had been standing beside Alfonso the whole time. He watched Alfonso's hands -- the obsidian catching the red light as the fingers moved.

"When you close your fingers, do you have to tell each knuckle to move? Or do they just obey?"

"They just obey. At this point it's like walking, or how breathing was. This is as much me as I ever have been. Sometimes more."

"I watch you, Alfonso, and I see the only other person in this company who knows what it feels like to wake up in a frame he wasn't born in."

He lifted his hand. It twitched, steadily -- nerve damage from the surgery. He pressed his thumb to the scar behind his ear.

"Does it ever hurt?"

"Nothing does," Alfonso said. "In a lot of ways I miss it. I feel like it's selfish to say I envy you. I don't think that's true, either. There's a lot you have that I don't, and the other way around."

"It's not hurting to not feel anything." Varon's voice stayed flat. "Years ago, in the mud of the Colony, the Argonian surgeons held me down on a cedar plank and cut me open. They shaved my jawbone down, and my orbitals. Clipped my ears. Softened my ribs. Pulled the skin tight and gave me this human face. They told me to hold on to the pain as an anchor to what I actually am, so I wouldn't forget. This mountain took twenty years off me in an instant, and all the old cuts opened back up. It all hurts like it's fresh. I'm living in a house that's already burned down.

"And I'm tethered to an amateur. Orion kills like a child playing a game. In the Warrens he left coin and bodies for the beggars to find. He showed his fangs to the little girl we look after. We were supposed to put Hroki down weeks ago, and instead we're out here playing politics, dragging our boots through the snow. My handler in the Brotherhood doesn't give extensions. My colony doesn't accept excuses. Every night I spend sitting with all of you, trying to get more time with Saijah, the debt gets heavier. Hroki is in Windhelm. If we don't get there and finish the job, the next contract might be for me.

"But enough of that. I don't mean to complain. Alfonso -- you studied at the College. I've tried healers, temple priests, alchemy, herbs, anything I could find. Restoration just makes more of the meat, and every time the tissue grows back, the scars get worse and the pain gets worse. You were smart enough to cheat death and put yourself back together without needing any flesh at all. You know things the temples couldn't dream of. Is there anything you can do to stop this? Please."

Alfonso did not answer right away.

"I want you to reconsider what you're asking. I'm not in the business of unconditional help, whether I'd like to be or not. Consider it a while longer. If you still decide the same, come back."

"I've considered it long enough. If you're willing to help me, I'll take it. I accept the cost."

Alfonso put out his hand. Varon shook it.

"Not right now," Alfonso said. "But I've got you."

Bigger Than All of It
Mila came up the terrace in her armor a moment later with the spear in both hands and Davinia behind her.

"Hi, Alfonso. Davinia's back, and I got a new outfit."

She turned all the way around so he could see it.

"Oh, you look so strong. That's a warrior right there."

He knelt, and asked her to kneel with him, and to hold out her hands palm up. He held his own above them and let the heat come into his bones -- the warmth that comes before a fire spell, without the fire.

"How does that feel?"

"That's a little hot."

"It is hot. I want you to feel it. Feel that heat in your hands, and try to imagine you can feel it all through your body. And I want you to tell fire to appear."

Mila shut her eyes. She thought about every time she had watched one of them do it -- Nora, Alfonso, the rest -- and she did what she had seen them do. What came out of her hands was a crack of lightning. It snapped across her own fingers and singed them, and the armor took the rest.

It hurt her. She looked up at him with her stinging hands still open.

"Now listen," Alfonso said. "You and that feeling are one and the same. That feeling hurts. I know it does. But that hurt, that pain, any fear you might feel for Saijah -- you are bigger than all of it. You will always be bigger than all of it. They're small parts of Mila. No part you could ever feel will be bigger than Mila herself. Does that make sense?"

"Yes."

"I'm proud of you."

She hugged him. Then she picked up the stone spear and walked off down the terrace on her own.

Davinia
"Good to see you," Alfonso said.

Davinia was looking at him the way she had looked at the vault, and at her own clothes, and at Bjorn's hands.

"When did that happen?"

"A while ago. Don't worry about it. I died a little bit."

"I was there for that."

"It was after that. We fought some guys, killed some guys, saw some bones. Liked them." He was quiet a moment. "I'll be honest, friend. I miss you. But there are a couple of things you've got to figure out for yourself."

Davinia looked down at the back of her hand, where his ember still glowed under the skin.

"She'll know the answer," Alfonso said. "You might, too. That's for you to decide. I'm just a skeleton."

Across the vault, the bronze doors stood a hand's width open on the dark beyond them, and nobody had gone near them yet.

